-ERA5 `ssrd` at time `t` is the accumulation over `(t − 1 h, t]`, whatever the time step of the downloaded data. The solar geometry is therefore computed at the centre of this window (`t − 30 min`). The length of the window is set by `climate.<source>.accumulation_window` (default `1h`). The top-of-atmosphere irradiance `SWtoa`, used for the clearness index, is the mean over the same window, including the Earth–Sun distance (see [Features](07_features.md)). Outputs downscaled by an older version of `TopoPyScale` with a solar file `ds_solar.nc` evaluated at the time stamps show a lag of about +30 min in `sw_timing`. The downscaling now computes the solar geometry itself, at the centre of the window.
+ERA5 `ssrd` at time `t` is the accumulation over `(t − 1 h, t]`. At 3 h or 6 h time steps, the store fetched by TopoPyScale holds the sums over `(t − 3 h, t]` or `(t − 6 h, t]` (store attribute `accumulation_hours`). The solar geometry is therefore computed at the centre of the window (`t − 30 min` for hourly data). The length of the window follows the store, or `climate.<source>.accumulation_window` when set. The top-of-atmosphere irradiance `SWtoa`, used for the clearness index, is the mean over the same window, including the Earth–Sun distance (see [Features](07_features.md)). Outputs downscaled by an older version of `TopoPyScale` with a solar file `ds_solar.nc` evaluated at the time stamps show a lag of about +30 min in `sw_timing`. The downscaling now computes the solar geometry itself, at the centre of the window.
 
